{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"state-stack-mutation-round-4","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State stack mutation round 4"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Revisions:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutation campaign: the 53 mutants ran on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"de8d0f1bf"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" after #2040)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Witnesses: the first versions ran on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2376eb581"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" after #2048). The\nfirst review's fixes ran on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d57be3d6f"},{"type":"text","value":", and its merge of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"5a24b9025"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Second review: its fixes are in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"7bc58cfab"},{"type":"text","value":", on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"5a24b9025"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Third review: its fixes are in the commit that adds this line, on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"7bc58cfab"},{"type":"text","value":". The X7 results ran on that commit."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This branch changes no production code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"method","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Round 4 applied 53 plausible maintenance mistakes to unchanged "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":": 23 in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", 15 in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", 6 in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutations.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", 4 in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lifecycle.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", 3 in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync-receipt.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", and one each in the lazy "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" getters of\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"live-query-observer.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"live-query-window-controller.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". Each mutant\nran against the full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" suite with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--bail=1"},{"type":"text","value":". Of the 53, 44\nfailed the suite and 9 survived. Each survivor received a probe that runs on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":", a code argument, or full-suite instrumentation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Triage also found a bug on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":". A sync transaction begun inside an open\none could commit first and make the rebuild lose last-write-wins row\nmetadata. #2048 fixes it, and its record corrects the round 3 claim that the\nhistory was unreachable."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"survivors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Survivors"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Change"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verdict"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SY8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"write"},{"type":"text","value":" stages writes into a transaction that a replay invalidated"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed here."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SY13"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A stale sync run's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed here."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A receipt with no acceptance moment counts as accepted at once"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed here."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The observer snapshot builds a new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" map on every read"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed here."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The window snapshot copies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" on every read"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap. Closed here."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ST27"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dropping a held row keeps its pending local origin"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent. The branch is unreachable. See below."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ST16"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recompute copies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowOrigins"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of keeping a reference"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent by argument. Recompute writes no origin before it reads the reference."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ST17"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The origin snapshot stores "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" for keys without an origin"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent. Every reader treats a missing origin as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remote"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SY18"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A missing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent. The caller resolves a non-promise result the same way."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"gaps-closed-here","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gaps closed here"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writes after invalidation (SY8)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" After a replay invalidates the open\ntransaction, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"write"},{"type":"text","value":" returns early. Without that guard, a late write enters\nthe pending projection, and a newer transaction classifies its own inserts\nagainst it. A probe on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" showed the effect. A late insert of key 9 made\na newer, valid insert of key 9 throw "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DuplicateKeySyncError"},{"type":"text","value":", and row 9 was\nlost. Two witnesses in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"\nwrite a late insert and a late delete. The newer transaction must see only\nthe accepted row. Both fail under SY8."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A stale run's commit (SY13)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The retention oracle's lifecycle driver\ncommitted from an old run only while that run was still current. Now the old\nrun also writes and commits a row after cleanup ends it, at two points: after\na plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restart"},{"type":"text","value":", and while a reentrant restart's new transaction is still\nopen. The commit must return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", and the row must not appear. The fixed\nand random retention campaigns fail under SY13. A mutant that routes a stale\nrun's write into the new run's open transaction fails both pinned\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"afterOldReturn"},{"type":"text","value":" histories, because the row appears. After a plain restart no\ntransaction is open, so there a stale write fails only by throwing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Acceptance of an abandoned receipt (X3)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A commit that core never\naccepts, because its signal was aborted or a replay invalidated it, returns a\nbare receipt with no acceptance moment. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenSyncAccepted"},{"type":"text","value":" must then wait for\nthat receipt, which rejects. The optimistic-history oracle's aborted batches\nand the retention oracle's invalidation replay now check that the acceptance\nmoment rejects too. Under X3, the invalidation replay, the open-at-settlement\nhistories, and the fixed and random optimistic-history campaigns fail. X3 also\nreaches every adapter that calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenSyncAccepted"},{"type":"text","value":" on a core receipt:\nSQLite persistence, Query DB, Electric, and PowerSync. Under X3 those callers\nwould treat an abandoned write as accepted. This review did not run their\nsuites under X3. The core witnesses above own the fallback rule."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State identity (X4, X5)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Before #2043, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" was a field of the\nsnapshot. The lazy getter must keep that identity: one snapshot has one\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" map. Consumers that compare by identity, such as a React dependency\nlist, rely on it. The snapshot oracle now checks that a second read of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the first read's objects. Five of its six tests fail\nunder X4. The sixth is the calibration test, which replaces the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":"\ngetter. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" is a plain field today, so its identity check has no\ndemonstrated kill. It guards a future lazy "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":". A witness in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"live-query-window-controller.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" checks the window snapshot and fails\nunder both X4 and X5."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second review found X6, which shares one map per observer and refills it\non every read. Each read then looks right on its own, so X6 passed. Now the\noracle saves every map it reads and checks each one again after all reads.\nSnapshots with different rows must also have different maps. The window\nwitness fetches a page and checks that the newer revision has its own map and\nthe older map is unchanged. Under X6, five of the six snapshot tests and the\nwindow witness fail."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A third review found X7, a single cache entry on the observer. It builds a\nnew map whenever a different snapshot is read, so a later read of an earlier\nsnapshot returns a new map with the same rows. X7 passed every check above.\nNow the oracle reads each snapshot again after all reads and requires the map\nthat its first read returned. The window witness reads the older snapshot\nagain after the newer one. Under X7, the same six tests fail."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"st27-an-unreachable-branch","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ST27: an unreachable branch"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"recomputeOptimisticState"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps a completed optimistic row only while a\ncommitted, queued sync transaction touches its key. It drops a held row whose\nkey no longer has such a transaction, and ST27 skips the origin delete in that\nbranch. A committed transaction leaves the queue only by applying, and the\ncommit path then clears the held row and the pending local origin for each of\nits keys. A cleanup reset clears both maps. So no reached history takes the\nbranch. Full-suite instrumentation agrees. A log write in the branch fired 0\ntimes across 249 files and 8,578 tests. A log write at the loop entry fired 55\ntimes in two of those files. The branch is a code-weight candidate. Its\nremoval belongs with the cuts, with this argument as its evidence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"other-finding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other finding"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The window snapshot's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" is the observer's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", so it includes the\npeek-ahead row that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" omits. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" in React returns that\nmap. This predates #2043. This review does not decide whether "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" should\nmatch "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A medium code review found eight items. Each is fixed here."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A commit hook's ESLint run removed a type assertion from the snapshot\noracle, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsc"},{"type":"text","value":" then failed. The setup now passes type arguments."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The record said X3 cannot reach the persistence packages. It can reach\nevery adapter that calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenSyncAccepted"},{"type":"text","value":". Corrected above."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The window witness did not clean up in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":" block. Fixed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The stale-run row check could not fail on its own after a plain restart.\nThe driver now also writes from the ended run while a new transaction is\nopen, and a routing mutant fails there."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" identity check has no demonstrated kill. Stated above."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reentrant restarts never reached the stale commit. Fixed with item 4."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The snapshot oracle has six tests, not five. Corrected."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The SY8 witnesses did not check their premise. They now check that the\nopen transaction is invalid before the late write."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"second-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Second review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A second code review, of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"5a24b9025"},{"type":"text","value":", found two items."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The record named the tested revision as \"this branch on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2376eb581"},{"type":"text","value":"\",\nbut later commits changed the witnesses. Fixed: the revisions above name\neach run."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X6, a map shared across snapshots, passed the identity checks. Fixed: the\nsaved-map and cross-revision checks under \"State identity\" above."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"third-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Third review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A third review, of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"7bc58cfab"},{"type":"text","value":", found one item. The saved-map checks did not\nread a snapshot again after another snapshot was read, so X7 passed. Fixed:\nthe identity checks under \"State identity\" above."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit then asked for the same recheck of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":". The oracle now saves\neach first read's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" too and requires it on the later read. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" is a\nplain field today, so this check has no demonstrated kill."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC outcomes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" SY8 follows the comment on the guard: a commit receipt\nowns the invalidation, and later writes cannot revive it. SY13 follows\nthe stale-run rule in the retention oracle's opening prose, which now\nstates the commit result. X3 follows the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenSyncAccepted"},{"type":"text","value":" contract. X4\nand X5 follow the snapshot oracle's point-in-time contract, which now\nstates identity."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The witnesses expect fixed outcomes or use the existing\nindependent models."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each change has prose beside its code."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restart"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reentrantRestart"},{"type":"text","value":" actions are in the\nexisting generated grammar. Every plain restart and every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"afterOldReturn"},{"type":"text","value":"\nreentrant restart reaches the stale commit. An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insideListener"},{"type":"text","value":" reentrant\nrestart commits the new run inside the listener, so no open transaction\nremains for a stale write to reach. The other changes add observations,\nnot grammar."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each witness drives a real Collection, observer, or\nwindow controller and observes public results."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each closed mutant fails the tests named above."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The retention and optimistic-history oracles run fixed\nand random campaigns with seed and path replay."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No model gained state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" \"Acceptance moment\" is the value "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenSyncAccepted"},{"type":"text","value":"\nreturns."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each witness cleans up its Collection, live query, or\ncontroller in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":" block."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: not applicable."}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012: met by this record."}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The SY8 witnesses cover both directions of the leak: one\nmakes a valid insert throw, and one lets a duplicate insert pass. Each\nfirst checks that the replay already invalidated the open transaction."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014: not applicable."}]}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"state-stack-mutation-round-4","text":"State stack mutation round 4","level":1},{"id":"method","text":"Method","level":2},{"id":"survivors","text":"Survivors","level":2},{"id":"gaps-closed-here","text":"Gaps closed here","level":2},{"id":"st27-an-unreachable-branch","text":"ST27: an unreachable branch","level":2},{"id":"other-finding","text":"Other finding","level":2},{"id":"review","text":"Review","level":2},{"id":"second-review","text":"Second review","level":2},{"id":"third-review","text":"Third review","level":2},{"id":"orc-outcomes","text":"ORC outcomes","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# State stack mutation round 4\n\nRevisions:\n\n- Mutation campaign: the 53 mutants ran on `de8d0f1bf` (`main` after #2040).\n- Witnesses: the first versions ran on `2376eb581` (`main` after #2048). The\n  first review's fixes ran on `d57be3d6f`, and its merge of `main` on\n  `5a24b9025`.\n- Second review: its fixes are in `7bc58cfab`, on `5a24b9025`.\n- Third review: its fixes are in the commit that adds this line, on\n  `7bc58cfab`. The X7 results ran on that commit.\n\nThis branch changes no production code.\n\n## Method\n\nRound 4 applied 53 plausible maintenance mistakes to unchanged `main`: 23 in\n`state.ts`, 15 in `sync.ts`, 6 in `mutations.ts`, 4 in `lifecycle.ts`, 3 in\n`sync-receipt.ts`, and one each in the lazy `state` getters of\n`live-query-observer.ts` and `live-query-window-controller.ts`. Each mutant\nran against the full `@tanstack/db` suite with `--bail=1`. Of the 53, 44\nfailed the suite and 9 survived. Each survivor received a probe that runs on\n`main`, a code argument, or full-suite instrumentation.\n\nTriage also found a bug on `main`. A sync transaction begun inside an open\none could commit first and make the rebuild lose last-write-wins row\nmetadata. #2048 fixes it, and its record corrects the round 3 claim that the\nhistory was unreachable.\n\n## Survivors\n\n| Mutant | Change | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SY8 | `write` stages writes into a transaction that a replay invalidated | Gap. Closed here. |\n| SY13 | A stale sync run's `commit` returns `false` | Gap. Closed here. |\n| X3 | A receipt with no acceptance moment counts as accepted at once | Gap. Closed here. |\n| X4 | The observer snapshot builds a new `state` map on every read | Gap. Closed here. |\n| X5 | The window snapshot copies `state` on every read | Gap. Closed here. |\n| ST27 | Dropping a held row keeps its pending local origin | Equivalent. The branch is unreachable. See below. |\n| ST16 | Recompute copies `rowOrigins` instead of keeping a reference | Equivalent by argument. Recompute writes no origin before it reads the reference. |\n| ST17 | The origin snapshot stores `undefined` for keys without an origin | Equivalent. Every reader treats a missing origin as `remote`. |\n| SY18 | A missing `loadSubset` returns `false` | Equivalent. The caller resolves a non-promise result the same way. |\n\n## Gaps closed here\n\n**Writes after invalidation (SY8).** After a replay invalidates the open\ntransaction, `write` returns early. Without that guard, a late write enters\nthe pending projection, and a newer transaction classifies its own inserts\nagainst it. A probe on `main` showed the effect. A late insert of key 9 made\na newer, valid insert of key 9 throw `DuplicateKeySyncError`, and row 9 was\nlost. Two witnesses in `collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts`\nwrite a late insert and a late delete. The newer transaction must see only\nthe accepted row. Both fail under SY8.\n\n**A stale run's commit (SY13).** The retention oracle's lifecycle driver\ncommitted from an old run only while that run was still current. Now the old\nrun also writes and commits a row after cleanup ends it, at two points: after\na plain `restart`, and while a reentrant restart's new transaction is still\nopen. The commit must return `true`, and the row must not appear. The fixed\nand random retention campaigns fail under SY13. A mutant that routes a stale\nrun's write into the new run's open transaction fails both pinned\n`afterOldReturn` histories, because the row appears. After a plain restart no\ntransaction is open, so there a stale write fails only by throwing.\n\n**Acceptance of an abandoned receipt (X3).** A commit that core never\naccepts, because its signal was aborted or a replay invalidated it, returns a\nbare receipt with no acceptance moment. `whenSyncAccepted` must then wait for\nthat receipt, which rejects. The optimistic-history oracle's aborted batches\nand the retention oracle's invalidation replay now check that the acceptance\nmoment rejects too. Under X3, the invalidation replay, the open-at-settlement\nhistories, and the fixed and random optimistic-history campaigns fail. X3 also\nreaches every adapter that calls `whenSyncAccepted` on a core receipt:\nSQLite persistence, Query DB, Electric, and PowerSync. Under X3 those callers\nwould treat an abandoned write as accepted. This review did not run their\nsuites under X3. The core witnesses above own the fallback rule.\n\n**State identity (X4, X5).** Before #2043, `state` was a field of the\nsnapshot. The lazy getter must keep that identity: one snapshot has one\n`state` map. Consumers that compare by identity, such as a React dependency\nlist, rely on it. The snapshot oracle now checks that a second read of `data`\nand `state` returns the first read's objects. Five of its six tests fail\nunder X4. The sixth is the calibration test, which replaces the `state`\ngetter. `data` is a plain field today, so its identity check has no\ndemonstrated kill. It guards a future lazy `data`. A witness in\n`live-query-window-controller.test.ts` checks the window snapshot and fails\nunder both X4 and X5.\n\nThe second review found X6, which shares one map per observer and refills it\non every read. Each read then looks right on its own, so X6 passed. Now the\noracle saves every map it reads and checks each one again after all reads.\nSnapshots with different rows must also have different maps. The window\nwitness fetches a page and checks that the newer revision has its own map and\nthe older map is unchanged. Under X6, five of the six snapshot tests and the\nwindow witness fail.\n\nA third review found X7, a single cache entry on the observer. It builds a\nnew map whenever a different snapshot is read, so a later read of an earlier\nsnapshot returns a new map with the same rows. X7 passed every check above.\nNow the oracle reads each snapshot again after all reads and requires the map\nthat its first read returned. The window witness reads the older snapshot\nagain after the newer one. Under X7, the same six tests fail.\n\n## ST27: an unreachable branch\n\n`recomputeOptimisticState` keeps a completed optimistic row only while a\ncommitted, queued sync transaction touches its key. It drops a held row whose\nkey no longer has such a transaction, and ST27 skips the origin delete in that\nbranch. A committed transaction leaves the queue only by applying, and the\ncommit path then clears the held row and the pending local origin for each of\nits keys. A cleanup reset clears both maps. So no reached history takes the\nbranch. Full-suite instrumentation agrees. A log write in the branch fired 0\ntimes across 249 files and 8,578 tests. A log write at the loop entry fired 55\ntimes in two of those files. The branch is a code-weight candidate. Its\nremoval belongs with the cuts, with this argument as its evidence.\n\n## Other finding\n\nThe window snapshot's `state` is the observer's `state`, so it includes the\npeek-ahead row that `data` omits. `useLiveInfiniteQuery` in React returns that\nmap. This predates #2043. This review does not decide whether `state` should\nmatch `data`.\n\n## Review\n\nA medium code review found eight items. Each is fixed here.\n\n1. A commit hook's ESLint run removed a type assertion from the snapshot\n   oracle, and `tsc` then failed. The setup now passes type arguments.\n2. The record said X3 cannot reach the persistence packages. It can reach\n   every adapter that calls `whenSyncAccepted`. Corrected above.\n3. The window witness did not clean up in a `finally` block. Fixed.\n4. The stale-run row check could not fail on its own after a plain restart.\n   The driver now also writes from the ended run while a new transaction is\n   open, and a routing mutant fails there.\n5. The `data` identity check has no demonstrated kill. Stated above.\n6. Reentrant restarts never reached the stale commit. Fixed with item 4.\n7. The snapshot oracle has six tests, not five. Corrected.\n8. The SY8 witnesses did not check their premise. They now check that the\n   open transaction is invalid before the late write.\n\n## Second review\n\nA second code review, of `5a24b9025`, found two items.\n\n1. The record named the tested revision as \"this branch on `2376eb581`\",\n   but later commits changed the witnesses. Fixed: the revisions above name\n   each run.\n2. X6, a map shared across snapshots, passed the identity checks. Fixed: the\n   saved-map and cross-revision checks under \"State identity\" above.\n\n## Third review\n\nA third review, of `7bc58cfab`, found one item. The saved-map checks did not\nread a snapshot again after another snapshot was read, so X7 passed. Fixed:\nthe identity checks under \"State identity\" above.\n\nCodeRabbit then asked for the same recheck of `data`. The oracle now saves\neach first read's `data` too and requires it on the later read. `data` is a\nplain field today, so this check has no demonstrated kill.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\n- **ORC-001: met.** SY8 follows the comment on the guard: a commit receipt\n  owns the invalidation, and later writes cannot revive it. SY13 follows\n  the stale-run rule in the retention oracle's opening prose, which now\n  states the commit result. X3 follows the `whenSyncAccepted` contract. X4\n  and X5 follow the snapshot oracle's point-in-time contract, which now\n  states identity.\n- **ORC-002: met.** The witnesses expect fixed outcomes or use the existing\n  independent models.\n- **ORC-003: met.** Each change has prose beside its code.\n- **ORC-004: met.** The `restart` and `reentrantRestart` actions are in the\n  existing generated grammar. Every plain restart and every `afterOldReturn`\n  reentrant restart reaches the stale commit. An `insideListener` reentrant\n  restart commits the new run inside the listener, so no open transaction\n  remains for a stale write to reach. The other changes add observations,\n  not grammar.\n- **ORC-005: met.** Each witness drives a real Collection, observer, or\n  window controller and observes public results.\n- **ORC-006: met.** Each closed mutant fails the tests named above.\n- **ORC-007: met.** The retention and optimistic-history oracles run fixed\n  and random campaigns with seed and path replay.\n- **ORC-008: not applicable.** No model gained state.\n- **ORC-009: met.** \"Acceptance moment\" is the value `whenSyncAccepted`\n  returns.\n- **ORC-010: met.** Each witness cleans up its Collection, live query, or\n  controller in a `finally` block.\n- **ORC-011: not applicable.**\n- **ORC-012: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013: met.** The SY8 witnesses cover both directions of the leak: one\n  makes a valid insert throw, and one lets a duplicate insert pass. Each\n  first checks that the replay already invalidated the open transaction.\n- **ORC-014: not applicable.**\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# State stack mutation round 4\n\nRevisions:\n\n- Mutation campaign: the 53 mutants ran on `de8d0f1bf` (`main` after #2040).\n- Witnesses: the first versions ran on `2376eb581` (`main` after #2048). The\n  first review's fixes ran on `d57be3d6f`, and its merge of `main` on\n  `5a24b9025`.\n- Second review: its fixes are in `7bc58cfab`, on `5a24b9025`.\n- Third review: its fixes are in the commit that adds this line, on\n  `7bc58cfab`. The X7 results ran on that commit.\n\nThis branch changes no production code.\n\n## Method\n\nRound 4 applied 53 plausible maintenance mistakes to unchanged `main`: 23 in\n`state.ts`, 15 in `sync.ts`, 6 in `mutations.ts`, 4 in `lifecycle.ts`, 3 in\n`sync-receipt.ts`, and one each in the lazy `state` getters of\n`live-query-observer.ts` and `live-query-window-controller.ts`. Each mutant\nran against the full `@tanstack/db` suite with `--bail=1`. Of the 53, 44\nfailed the suite and 9 survived. Each survivor received a probe that runs on\n`main`, a code argument, or full-suite instrumentation.\n\nTriage also found a bug on `main`. A sync transaction begun inside an open\none could commit first and make the rebuild lose last-write-wins row\nmetadata. #2048 fixes it, and its record corrects the round 3 claim that the\nhistory was unreachable.\n\n## Survivors\n\n| Mutant | Change | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SY8 | `write` stages writes into a transaction that a replay invalidated | Gap. Closed here. |\n| SY13 | A stale sync run's `commit` returns `false` | Gap. Closed here. |\n| X3 | A receipt with no acceptance moment counts as accepted at once | Gap. Closed here. |\n| X4 | The observer snapshot builds a new `state` map on every read | Gap. Closed here. |\n| X5 | The window snapshot copies `state` on every read | Gap. Closed here. |\n| ST27 | Dropping a held row keeps its pending local origin | Equivalent. The branch is unreachable. See below. |\n| ST16 | Recompute copies `rowOrigins` instead of keeping a reference | Equivalent by argument. Recompute writes no origin before it reads the reference. |\n| ST17 | The origin snapshot stores `undefined` for keys without an origin | Equivalent. Every reader treats a missing origin as `remote`. |\n| SY18 | A missing `loadSubset` returns `false` | Equivalent. The caller resolves a non-promise result the same way. |\n\n## Gaps closed here\n\n**Writes after invalidation (SY8).** After a replay invalidates the open\ntransaction, `write` returns early. Without that guard, a late write enters\nthe pending projection, and a newer transaction classifies its own inserts\nagainst it. A probe on `main` showed the effect. A late insert of key 9 made\na newer, valid insert of key 9 throw `DuplicateKeySyncError`, and row 9 was\nlost. Two witnesses in `collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts`\nwrite a late insert and a late delete. The newer transaction must see only\nthe accepted row. Both fail under SY8.\n\n**A stale run's commit (SY13).** The retention oracle's lifecycle driver\ncommitted from an old run only while that run was still current. Now the old\nrun also writes and commits a row after cleanup ends it, at two points: after\na plain `restart`, and while a reentrant restart's new transaction is still\nopen. The commit must return `true`, and the row must not appear. The fixed\nand random retention campaigns fail under SY13. A mutant that routes a stale\nrun's write into the new run's open transaction fails both pinned\n`afterOldReturn` histories, because the row appears. After a plain restart no\ntransaction is open, so there a stale write fails only by throwing.\n\n**Acceptance of an abandoned receipt (X3).** A commit that core never\naccepts, because its signal was aborted or a replay invalidated it, returns a\nbare receipt with no acceptance moment. `whenSyncAccepted` must then wait for\nthat receipt, which rejects. The optimistic-history oracle's aborted batches\nand the retention oracle's invalidation replay now check that the acceptance\nmoment rejects too. Under X3, the invalidation replay, the open-at-settlement\nhistories, and the fixed and random optimistic-history campaigns fail. X3 also\nreaches every adapter that calls `whenSyncAccepted` on a core receipt:\nSQLite persistence, Query DB, Electric, and PowerSync. Under X3 those callers\nwould treat an abandoned write as accepted. This review did not run their\nsuites under X3. The core witnesses above own the fallback rule.\n\n**State identity (X4, X5).** Before #2043, `state` was a field of the\nsnapshot. The lazy getter must keep that identity: one snapshot has one\n`state` map. Consumers that compare by identity, such as a React dependency\nlist, rely on it. The snapshot oracle now checks that a second read of `data`\nand `state` returns the first read's objects. Five of its six tests fail\nunder X4. The sixth is the calibration test, which replaces the `state`\ngetter. `data` is a plain field today, so its identity check has no\ndemonstrated kill. It guards a future lazy `data`. A witness in\n`live-query-window-controller.test.ts` checks the window snapshot and fails\nunder both X4 and X5.\n\nThe second review found X6, which shares one map per observer and refills it\non every read. Each read then looks right on its own, so X6 passed. Now the\noracle saves every map it reads and checks each one again after all reads.\nSnapshots with different rows must also have different maps. The window\nwitness fetches a page and checks that the newer revision has its own map and\nthe older map is unchanged. Under X6, five of the six snapshot tests and the\nwindow witness fail.\n\nA third review found X7, a single cache entry on the observer. It builds a\nnew map whenever a different snapshot is read, so a later read of an earlier\nsnapshot returns a new map with the same rows. X7 passed every check above.\nNow the oracle reads each snapshot again after all reads and requires the map\nthat its first read returned. The window witness reads the older snapshot\nagain after the newer one. Under X7, the same six tests fail.\n\n## ST27: an unreachable branch\n\n`recomputeOptimisticState` keeps a completed optimistic row only while a\ncommitted, queued sync transaction touches its key. It drops a held row whose\nkey no longer has such a transaction, and ST27 skips the origin delete in that\nbranch. A committed transaction leaves the queue only by applying, and the\ncommit path then clears the held row and the pending local origin for each of\nits keys. A cleanup reset clears both maps. So no reached history takes the\nbranch. Full-suite instrumentation agrees. A log write in the branch fired 0\ntimes across 249 files and 8,578 tests. A log write at the loop entry fired 55\ntimes in two of those files. The branch is a code-weight candidate. Its\nremoval belongs with the cuts, with this argument as its evidence.\n\n## Other finding\n\nThe window snapshot's `state` is the observer's `state`, so it includes the\npeek-ahead row that `data` omits. `useLiveInfiniteQuery` in React returns that\nmap. This predates #2043. This review does not decide whether `state` should\nmatch `data`.\n\n## Review\n\nA medium code review found eight items. Each is fixed here.\n\n1. A commit hook's ESLint run removed a type assertion from the snapshot\n   oracle, and `tsc` then failed. The setup now passes type arguments.\n2. The record said X3 cannot reach the persistence packages. It can reach\n   every adapter that calls `whenSyncAccepted`. Corrected above.\n3. The window witness did not clean up in a `finally` block. Fixed.\n4. The stale-run row check could not fail on its own after a plain restart.\n   The driver now also writes from the ended run while a new transaction is\n   open, and a routing mutant fails there.\n5. The `data` identity check has no demonstrated kill. Stated above.\n6. Reentrant restarts never reached the stale commit. Fixed with item 4.\n7. The snapshot oracle has six tests, not five. Corrected.\n8. The SY8 witnesses did not check their premise. They now check that the\n   open transaction is invalid before the late write.\n\n## Second review\n\nA second code review, of `5a24b9025`, found two items.\n\n1. The record named the tested revision as \"this branch on `2376eb581`\",\n   but later commits changed the witnesses. Fixed: the revisions above name\n   each run.\n2. X6, a map shared across snapshots, passed the identity checks. Fixed: the\n   saved-map and cross-revision checks under \"State identity\" above.\n\n## Third review\n\nA third review, of `7bc58cfab`, found one item. The saved-map checks did not\nread a snapshot again after another snapshot was read, so X7 passed. Fixed:\nthe identity checks under \"State identity\" above.\n\nCodeRabbit then asked for the same recheck of `data`. The oracle now saves\neach first read's `data` too and requires it on the later read. `data` is a\nplain field today, so this check has no demonstrated kill.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\n- **ORC-001: met.** SY8 follows the comment on the guard: a commit receipt\n  owns the invalidation, and later writes cannot revive it. SY13 follows\n  the stale-run rule in the retention oracle's opening prose, which now\n  states the commit result. X3 follows the `whenSyncAccepted` contract. X4\n  and X5 follow the snapshot oracle's point-in-time contract, which now\n  states identity.\n- **ORC-002: met.** The witnesses expect fixed outcomes or use the existing\n  independent models.\n- **ORC-003: met.** Each change has prose beside its code.\n- **ORC-004: met.** The `restart` and `reentrantRestart` actions are in the\n  existing generated grammar. Every plain restart and every `afterOldReturn`\n  reentrant restart reaches the stale commit. An `insideListener` reentrant\n  restart commits the new run inside the listener, so no open transaction\n  remains for a stale write to reach. The other changes add observations,\n  not grammar.\n- **ORC-005: met.** Each witness drives a real Collection, observer, or\n  window controller and observes public results.\n- **ORC-006: met.** Each closed mutant fails the tests named above.\n- **ORC-007: met.** The retention and optimistic-history oracles run fixed\n  and random campaigns with seed and path replay.\n- **ORC-008: not applicable.** No model gained state.\n- **ORC-009: met.** \"Acceptance moment\" is the value `whenSyncAccepted`\n  returns.\n- **ORC-010: met.** Each witness cleans up its Collection, live query, or\n  controller in a `finally` block.\n- **ORC-011: not applicable.**\n- **ORC-012: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013: met.** The SY8 witnesses cover both directions of the leak: one\n  makes a valid insert throw, and one lets a duplicate insert pass. Each\n  first checks that the replay already invalidated the open transaction.\n- **ORC-014: not applicable.**\n"}
